#
# ThreadSanitizer suppressions, read by `scripts/tsan-gate.sh`.
#
# EVERY ENTRY IS A KNOWN BUG THAT IS STILL OPEN, and must name the issue that
# tracks it. This file is not a place to make a report go away; it is the list of
# races the gate already knows about, so that the gate can be red for a NEW one.
# An entry with no issue number is a defect in this file.
#
# Removing an entry is part of fixing its issue. If a fix lands and the entry
# stays, the next regression of that exact race is invisible -- which is the
# failure mode this whole gate exists to prevent, reintroduced one layer up.
#
# ## Scope, and why it is worth stating
#
# Prefer `race_top:` to `race:`. `race:` matches a function name ANYWHERE in either
# stack, so it also silences a future, unrelated race that merely passes through
# that function -- and a teardown path like `Close()` is traversed by a great many
# stacks. `race_top:` matches only the frame the racy access is actually in, which
# is the report being suppressed and nothing wider. If a `race_top:` entry stops
# matching, the accessing frame has been inlined: name the inlining frame, do not
# widen back to `race:`.
# `scripts/tsan-gate.sh` prints ThreadSanitizer's own "Matched N suppressions"
# line on every run, so a suppression that starts matching more than it did is at
# least visible rather than silent.
#
# The gate additionally proves this file is not swallowing everything: the
# canary's race is checked WITH these suppressions active, so a pattern broad
# enough to silence the canary fails the gate.
#
# Format: https://clang.llvm.org/docs/ThreadSanitizerFlags.html

# https://github.com/LASTRADA-Software/fastcached/issues/260
#
# `AdminEndpoint` closes its listener from the main thread while its own accept
# thread is still inside `Accept()`. Reported 6 times from one underlying race,
# entirely within `AdminEndpoint` construction and teardown. This is the shape the
# rulebook already records for sockets -- "Close() can be the last thing that runs
# on a socket, so it must touch no member after it completes an awaitable" --
# arriving from the thread side rather than the coroutine side.
#
# Found by the first ThreadSanitizer run ever done on this tree, which is the
# argument for this gate existing at all.
race_top:FastCache::BlockingListener::Close
